WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
A receptor activates Ras without ever touching it.

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.120
How does the message cross the gap?

3
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.780
Watch the blue receptor and purple Ras.

4
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.760
Both sit at the membrane, but nothing yet connects them.

5
00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:21.330
A growth factor makes the receptor add phosphate marks to its tail.

6
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.130
Each gold mark is a possible docking site.

7
00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.890
Yet a phosphate cannot activate Ras by itself.

8
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:34.853
The receptor and Ras are separate proteins, each free to drift in the membrane.

9
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:40.093
The missing part must bind the receptor without consuming its phosphate.

10
00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:44.173
It must also carry something that can act on Ras.

11
00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:49.713
The reader is Grb2, a green adaptor with no enzyme activity.

12
00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:55.013
Its central SH2 domain is a cup for phosphotyrosine.

13
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:58.413
Its SH3 domains are paired grips.

14
00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:02.893
Those grips already hold the orange exchange factor Sos.

15
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:09.883
Which part should face the phosphorylated receptor: the SH2 cup or an SH3 grip?

16
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:14.683
Keep your choice in mind while the two shapes move toward the gold phosphate.

17
00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:25.167
The SH2 cup closes around the receptor phosphotyrosine.

18
00:01:26.427 --> 00:01:28.747
The SH3 grips keep holding Sos.

19
00:01:29.887 --> 00:01:33.967
The complete Grb2 and Sos unit moves to the receptor tail.

20
00:01:35.327 --> 00:01:38.547
The adaptor has connected a marked receptor to an enzyme.

21
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:42.827
Membrane-bound Ras now meets the recruited Sos.

22
00:01:43.947 --> 00:01:46.447
Sos is a guanine-nucleotide exchange factor.

23
00:01:47.687 --> 00:01:51.527
It releases GDP from Ras, allowing GTP to bind.

24
00:01:52.827 --> 00:01:54.867
Purple Ras changes from off to on.

25
00:01:56.187 --> 00:01:58.747
Grb2 never catalyzes that exchange.

26
00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:03.577
Recruitment changes location, not chemical identity.

27
00:02:05.077 --> 00:02:08.357
Sos was already attached to Grb2 in the cytoplasm.

28
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:14.717
After the SH2 cup docks, the same Sos sits at the membrane, where it can meet Ras.

29
00:02:16.117 --> 00:02:18.857
Positioning has become a decisive signaling step.

30
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.260
Sos also has another Ras-binding site, away from the exchange site.

31
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.340
Ras with GTP can bind there and speed Sos.

32
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.340
One activated Ras can therefore help Sos activate more, once recruitment starts.

33
00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:37.093
Now follow the route once.

34
00:02:38.353 --> 00:02:40.213
The receptor recruits Grb2.

35
00:02:41.493 --> 00:02:44.613
Grb2 brings Sos to the membrane, where Ras sits.

36
00:02:46.053 --> 00:02:48.693
Sos performs the exchange that activates Ras.

37
00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:54.153
Binding controls the encounter, while Sos performs the chemical work.

38
00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:59.987
The missing link from the opening is filled, but the receptor still never touches

39
00:02:59.987 --> 00:03:03.847
Ras. The adaptor changes who meets whom and where.

40
00:03:05.087 --> 00:03:09.607
It lets the right enzyme reach its target without the adaptor being an enzyme

41
00:03:09.607 --> 00:03:09.967
itself.
